# Target C-II — the vanishing-mass fully loaded force corridor shares a near-full dense train grid

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the no-positive-density-TB1 branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** vanishing-mass growing exact-force corridor; near-full-shore deep force blocks; exact train crossing-neighbor floor; adjacent-strip terminal-topology bound; exact train-step metric; source circumdisk capacity  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_vanishing_mass_dense_force_grid_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The selected vanishing-mass force corridor already inherits the global `B_0` inverse-`D` scaffold from its parent deep-force block. The concrete diagonal permits substantially more. Because every interface of the corridor has controlled terminal-topology cost and all rows have exactly one phase, the whole growing corridor can be crossed by **almost the full `0.6536D` train neighborhood** of one row. After deleting `o(D)` columns, every surviving rhombus in the complete growing rectangle has angular defect `o(1)`.

The exact train-step metric then gives two common transverse column flanks, simultaneously on every force-loaded row, lying at least `0.01D` on opposite sides of the source circumcenter coordinate. Each flank has density

\[
0.6536-{1.02\over\sqrt3}-o(1)
=0.0647027254\ldots-o(1).
\]

Thus the same corridor now simultaneously has:

- force loading at every separator;
- vanishing total original switching KKT mass;
- a uniformly coercive full cancellation kernel;
- a common near-equilateral grid with `0.6536D-o(D)` columns; and
- common circumcenter flanks of density `0.06470D-o(D)` on both sides.

This removes the possibility that the vanishing-mass corridor lives in a thin or poorly synchronized train neighborhood.

---

## 1. Use the concrete growing-force schedule

Retain the notation of the vanishing-mass corridor theorem. For the concrete diagonal,

\[
r_n=n,
\qquad
m_n=2n+1,
\tag{DG.1}
\]

\[
T_{0,n}=41n,
\qquad
T_{r,n}=41m_nn,
\tag{DG.2}
\]

and every interface of the selected block

\[
J_n=[a_n,b_n],
\qquad |J_n|=m_n,
\tag{DG.3}
\]

is `T_{r,n}`-good and exact-antipodal. All `m_n+1` rows have one exact oriented phase.

Let

\[
C=1{,}109{,}000,
\qquad
r_0=0.6536.
\tag{DG.4}
\]

For every row `tau_j` in the selected high-`t` shore, let `Gamma_j` be its complete-train crossing family. The audited crossing-neighbor floor gives

\[
\boxed{|\Gamma_j|>r_0D_n-o(D_n).}
\tag{DG.5}
\]

At every internal interface, `T_{r,n}`-goodness gives

\[
L_j<T_{r,n}C,
\tag{DG.6}
\]

where adjacent crossing families obey

\[
|\Gamma_j\triangle\Gamma_{j+1}|\le L_j.
\tag{DG.7}
\]

In the diagonal extraction we may enlarge `D_n` after all fixed-`n` thresholds have been met. Impose in addition

\[
\boxed{
{m_nT_{r,n}C\over D_n}\longrightarrow0.
}
\tag{DG.8}
\]

This is compatible with every earlier requirement, including the resolved-force condition `rho_nD_n^(1/4)->infinity` and the vanishing-mass estimates.

---

## 2. Almost the full train neighborhood crosses every row

Define the common crossing family

\[
P_n^{raw}
:=\bigcap_{j=a_n}^{b_n+1}\Gamma_j.
\tag{DG.9}
\]

Starting from the first row and passing through the block, at most `L_j` trains can be lost from the running intersection at interface `j`. Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
|P_n^{raw}|
&\ge |\Gamma_{a_n}|-
\sum_{j=a_n}^{b_n}L_j\\
&>r_0D_n-m_nT_{r,n}C-o(D_n).
\end{aligned}
\tag{DG.10}
\]

By (DG.8),

\[
\boxed{
|P_n^{raw}|>(0.6536-o(1))D_n.
}
\tag{DG.11}

Every two columns in `P_n^{raw}` both cross the first selected row. The complete-train crossing graph is triangle-free, so the columns are internally noncrossing. The selected rows are internally noncrossing by construction. Therefore the block and `P_n^{raw}` form one complete two-family pseudoline grid with a common crossing order.

This common family is more than eight times denser than the fixed global `B_0` family used by the parent force-grid theorem.

---

## 3. Exact co-phase lets one-row defect purification work on every row

Let `S_j` be the full strict-rhombus angular-defect sum on row `tau_j`. The center row of every parent force block is `T_{0,n}`-good, so for the center `i_n` of the selected block,

\[
S_{i_n}<T_{0,n}C.
\tag{DG.12}
\]

Every row in the corridor has exactly the same oriented phase as `tau_{i_n}`. For a fixed complete train column `b`, the oriented crossing defect has the phase form

\[
\delta_{jb}
=\beta_b-\alpha_j-{\pi\over3}.
\tag{DG.13}
\]

Since all `alpha_j` are equal on the exact-antipodal corridor,

\[
\boxed{
\delta_{jb}=\delta_{i_nb}
}
\tag{DG.14}
\]

whenever `b` crosses both rows. Thus a column defect estimate made on the center row automatically holds on **every** row of the corridor.

Set

\[
\sigma_n
:=\sqrt{{T_{0,n}C\over D_n}}.
\tag{DG.15}
\]

Enlarge `D_n` further if needed so

\[
\sigma_n\to0.
\tag{DG.16}
\]

Delete from `P_n^{raw}` every column with center-row defect greater than `sigma_n`. By (DG.12), fewer than

\[
{T_{0,n}C\over\sigma_n}
=\sigma_nD_n
=o(D_n)
\tag{DG.17}
\]

columns are deleted. Call the survivors `P_n`. Then

\[
\boxed{
|P_n|>(0.6536-o(1))D_n
}
\tag{DG.18}
\]

and, uniformly over the entire growing rectangle,

\[
\boxed{
0<\theta(\tau_j,b)-{\pi\over3}
\le\sigma_n=o(1)
}
\tag{DG.19}
\]

for every selected row `j` and every `b in P_n`.

So the vanishing-mass force corridor lies in a complete

\[
(m_n+1)\times(0.6536-o(1))D_n
\]

near-equilateral train rectangle whose row depth tends to infinity.

---

## 4. The same two column flanks bracket the circumcenter on every row

Fix

\[
\mu=0.01.
\tag{DG.20}
\]

For an oriented row `a`, write

\[
h_a(x)=\det(u_a,x-o)
\]

for the row-normal coordinate about the common source circumcenter `o`. Consecutive selected crossing-rhombus centers on a row are separated in this coordinate by more than

\[
{\sqrt3\over2},
\tag{DG.21}
\]

while all crossing centers lie in the source circumdisk of radius

\[
R_D={D_n\over2}+o(D_n).
\tag{DG.22}
\]

Therefore at most

\[
\left({1.02\over\sqrt3}+o(1)\right)D_n
\tag{DG.23}
\]

columns can have crossing center on either prescribed one-sided interval reaching from the circumcenter strip to the corresponding circumdisk boundary.

Since `P_n` has density `0.6536-o(1)`, each row has more than

\[
q_0D_n-o(D_n)
\tag{DG.24}
\]

crossings below `-0.01D_n` and the same number above `+0.01D_n`, where

\[
\boxed{
q_0
:=0.6536-{1.02\over\sqrt3}
=0.0647027254265\ldots .
}
\tag{DG.25}
\]

The crossing order of `P_n` is common on all rows, and `h_a` is monotone in that order. Hence the same initial and final column blocks work simultaneously on every row. There are common subfamilies

\[
P_n^-,P_n^+\subset P_n
\tag{DG.26}
\]

with

\[
\boxed{
|P_n^-|,|P_n^+|>(q_0-o(1))D_n
}
\tag{DG.27}
\]

such that every crossing center from `P_n^-` lies below the `-0.01D_n` row-normal level and every crossing center from `P_n^+` lies above the `+0.01D_n` level, simultaneously for every row in the force corridor.

---

## 5. Combined corridor normal form

Combine this theorem with the preceding vanishing-mass and coercive-kernel results. Along one retained subsequence there is a growing exact-phase corridor with

\[
m_n=2n+1\to\infty
\]

such that:

1. every internal interface carries a bilateral exact positive diameter-force pair;
2. every force ray has transverse component at least `c_H-o(1)=0.453819...-o(1)`;
3. the force pair is centrally resolved above the `D_n^(3/4)` midpoint scale;
4. the total original switching KKT mass over **all** internal interfaces tends to zero;
5. after refining the diagonal, the total compressed two-ray coefficient mass also tends to zero;
6. the normalized cut-resultant path has diameter tending to zero;
7. the complete full-support cancellation kernel has dimension `m_n` and normalized Euclidean derivative singular-value floor
   \[
   \sqrt2c_H-o(1)=0.641798\ldots-o(1);
   \]
8. every row crosses the same `(0.6536-o(1))D_n` internally noncrossing columns;
9. every one of those row-column rhombi has angle `pi/3+o(1)` uniformly; and
10. the same two column flanks of density `0.0647027D_n-o(D_n)` bracket the source circumcenter by `+-0.01D_n` on every row.

All ten properties hold in one corridor; no independent diagonalization between the force, stress, coercivity, and grid statements is needed.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The no-TB1 survivor can no longer place its growing, KKT-light, uniformly coercive force stack in a sparse or drifting train environment. It must realize that stack inside one growing near-equilateral pseudoline rectangle with a positive-linear transverse shore and common metric circumcenter brackets.

The remaining closure problem is correspondingly geometric: show that a source of diameter `D_n` cannot simultaneously support

```text
an unbounded stack of exact-co-phased O--O rows,
0.6536D_n common near-60-degree transverse trains,
common +/-0.01D_n circumcenter flanks,
a resolved bilateral diameter dipole at every row separator,
vanishing total switching KKT mass,
and a dimension-free coercivity gap on the whole cancellation kernel.
```

No current theorem converts this combined normal form into an exact unit triangle or a strict sub-`D` competitor. In particular, the train-grid metric controls crossing-center locations but does not yet determine where each diameter chord crosses each row strongly enough to force an endpoint contradiction.

**Disposition:** synchronized the vanishing-mass fully loaded force corridor with an almost-full common train neighborhood, uniform `o(1)` rhombus defect, and common positive-density circumcenter flanks. C-II and Level I remain open.
